1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,833
Good day.

2
00:00:01,584 --> 00:00:06,503
This is Figure 5, the Entity Relationship
Diagram for IronGrind.

3
00:00:07,273 --> 00:00:08,766
Each box is a table.

4
00:00:09,541 --> 00:00:14,274
The fields inside it are columns, and each
row in the database is a record.

5
00:00:15,037 --> 00:00:17,923
A primary key uniquely identifies a row.

6
00:00:18,662 --> 00:00:21,111
A foreign key references a related row.

7
00:00:21,863 --> 00:00:27,751
We will follow one example, from Ana applying
for membership to an administrator generating

8
00:00:27,751 --> 00:00:28,334
a report.

9
00:00:29,119 --> 00:00:35,018
At each step, we will see what gets stored,
where it goes, and why the records are

10
00:00:35,018 --> 00:00:35,522
linked.

11
00:00:37,597 --> 00:00:42,209
Ana fills out the online registration form
and selects a membership plan.

12
00:00:43,016 --> 00:00:45,962
Supabase Auth manages her login identity.

13
00:00:46,738 --> 00:00:50,679
Her application account details and role
belong in profiles.

14
00:00:51,457 --> 00:00:55,392
The profile ID references the user ID in
Supabase Auth.

15
00:00:56,292 --> 00:01:02,084
Her membership request, including the
selected plan and its review status, belongs

16
00:01:02,084 --> 00:01:03,991
in membership_applications.

17
00:01:04,763 --> 00:01:09,557
This lets the gym track whether the request
is pending, approved, or rejected.

18
00:01:10,324 --> 00:01:15,653
If a reviewer is recorded, the reviewed_by
field points to that reviewer’s profile.

19
00:01:17,722 --> 00:01:23,624
When Ana’s first registration is approved and
her member record is created, her current

20
00:01:23,624 --> 00:01:29,050
membership details, such as her plan and
expiration date, are stored in members.

21
00:01:29,798 --> 00:01:32,692
Look at application_id on the members table.

22
00:01:33,460 --> 00:01:36,691
It points to the ID in
membership_applications.

23
00:01:37,450 --> 00:01:42,767
If you start with Ana’s member record, this
link lets you find the application that led

24
00:01:42,767 --> 00:01:43,745
to its creation.

25
00:01:45,837 --> 00:01:48,250
Now suppose Ana requests a renewal.

26
00:01:49,022 --> 00:01:54,469
She already has a member record, so the new
request can reference her existing record.

27
00:01:55,234 --> 00:01:59,702
member_id in membership_applications points
to the ID in members.

28
00:02:00,461 --> 00:02:04,082
The two links between these tables answer
different questions.

29
00:02:04,847 --> 00:02:09,165
application_id on the member identifies the
original application.

30
00:02:09,946 --> 00:02:14,621
member_id on a request identifies which
member that request concerns.

31
00:02:16,673 --> 00:02:21,374
To check Ana’s current plan and expiry date,
open her members record.

32
00:02:22,137 --> 00:02:28,171
To see her recorded membership events, such
as a renewal, look in membership_history.

33
00:02:28,940 --> 00:02:32,466
The member_id on each history record points
to members.

34
00:02:33,222 --> 00:02:36,393
A member can have several history rows over
time.

35
00:02:37,181 --> 00:02:41,805
This is why the current membership and the
historical events have separate tables.

36
00:02:43,869 --> 00:02:45,342
Ana goes to the gym.

37
00:02:46,137 --> 00:02:51,097
Her visit is recorded in attendance, which
holds the check-in and the check-out when it

38
00:02:51,097 --> 00:02:51,838
is available.

39
00:02:52,593 --> 00:02:56,777
member_id on the attendance row points to
Ana’s ID in members.

40
00:02:57,537 --> 00:02:58,924
This is one to many.

41
00:02:59,692 --> 00:03:04,336
A member can have several visits, and each
visit belongs to one member.

42
00:03:05,089 --> 00:03:10,385
If Ana visits today and again tomorrow, the
two visit rows have different attendance

43
00:03:10,385 --> 00:03:12,970
identifiers and share the same member_id.

44
00:03:15,037 --> 00:03:19,571
For phone-based attendance scanning, the
database includes a table called

45
00:03:19,571 --> 00:03:21,620
mobile_attendance_pairing_policy.

46
00:03:22,397 --> 00:03:25,302
This holds the settings that govern pairing a
phone.

47
00:03:26,094 --> 00:03:31,671
If you are looking up Ana’s gym visit or her
check-in time, that record is in attendance.

48
00:03:32,408 --> 00:03:36,630
If you are looking up the pairing settings,
use the policy table.

49
00:03:38,689 --> 00:03:42,856
mobile_attendance_scan_sessions represents
paired scanner sessions.

50
00:03:43,618 --> 00:03:48,328
mobile_attendance_scan_events is the table
for individual scan event records.

51
00:03:49,111 --> 00:03:52,764
The session_id in an event points to the ID
of its session.

52
00:03:53,575 --> 00:03:57,008
Several recorded events can share the same
session_id.

53
00:03:57,770 --> 00:04:01,479
This link tells us which scanner session an
event belongs to.

54
00:04:02,213 --> 00:04:06,715
The member_id in attendance tells us which
member a gym visit belongs to.

55
00:04:08,756 --> 00:04:13,661
After visiting the gym, Ana buys one shirt
and two units of a supplement.

56
00:04:14,440 --> 00:04:18,684
The products table holds item details,
prices, and stock.

57
00:04:19,475 --> 00:04:21,344
Each product has its own ID.

58
00:04:22,113 --> 00:04:28,036
The sale line can use that ID to identify the
specific item sold, and the sales workflow

59
00:04:28,036 --> 00:04:30,055
can update the relevant stock.

60
00:04:32,155 --> 00:04:36,121
One row in sales represents the entire
checkout and its total.

61
00:04:36,884 --> 00:04:41,940
If the purchase is linked to Ana, the
member_id on the sale points to her member

62
00:04:41,940 --> 00:04:42,382
record.

63
00:04:43,155 --> 00:04:45,824
The individual items go in sale_items.

64
00:04:46,598 --> 00:04:48,884
The first row represents one shirt.

65
00:04:49,723 --> 00:04:52,928
The second row represents two units of the
supplement.

66
00:04:53,748 --> 00:04:57,937
Both rows share the same sale_id because they
belong to one checkout.

67
00:04:58,744 --> 00:05:03,022
They have different product IDs because they
refer to different products.

68
00:05:03,882 --> 00:05:08,738
Follow the sale_id to find the transaction
and the product_id to find the item.

69
00:05:10,837 --> 00:05:14,901
The administrator generates a sales report
for a selected period.

70
00:05:15,691 --> 00:05:19,472
The system uses the relevant sales records to
build the summary.

71
00:05:20,255 --> 00:05:26,633
When the report is recorded, its type,
period, format, and data are saved in

72
00:05:26,633 --> 00:05:28,143
generated_reports.

73
00:05:28,891 --> 00:05:32,482
The generated_by field points to the ID in
profiles.

74
00:05:33,247 --> 00:05:36,076
This link identifies who generated the
report.

75
00:05:36,923 --> 00:05:40,948
One profile can be associated with multiple
saved reports.

76
00:05:42,989 --> 00:05:47,165
activity_logs keeps a record of system
actions for later review.

77
00:05:47,943 --> 00:05:53,440
For example, the function that saves a
generated report can also record an audit

78
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:53,852
event.

79
00:05:54,611 --> 00:05:58,940
To answer which report was saved, look in
generated_reports.

80
00:05:59,680 --> 00:06:04,439
To answer which system action was recorded,
look in activity_logs.

81
00:06:06,493 --> 00:06:10,566
To recap, Ana’s request is in
membership_applications.

82
00:06:11,346 --> 00:06:13,657
Her current membership is in members.

83
00:06:14,438 --> 00:06:18,922
Her recorded changes are in
membership_history, and her visits are in

84
00:06:18,922 --> 00:06:19,637
attendance.

85
00:06:20,394 --> 00:06:25,302
Her checkout is in sales, with the specific
purchases in sale_items.

86
00:06:26,061 --> 00:06:29,628
A saved report can be traced to its creator’s
profile.

87
00:06:30,384 --> 00:06:36,301
To explain a line in the ERD, name the
foreign key, name the key it references, and

88
00:06:36,301 --> 00:06:39,367
explain what the relationship lets us find.

